rietary methods or create a target for attackers (though it's an evaluation benchmark, not the system itself). More importantly, \"maintaining versioned updates tracking amendments from national implementing measures\" is a huge administrative burden on the compliance/legal team.\n\n        *   Does this solve Article 12? It doesn't *implement* the logging in our specific high-risk systems (which is what the law requires), but it creates a framework to *verify* that implementation.\n        *   Is it \"scarce resource\" worthy? Building a public benchmark is a significant engineering and legal effort. Is the reputation gain worth the cost relative to just implementing the logs for our own products?\n\n    *   The prompt asks me as the Compliance Officer. My main concerns are survival, compliance, and reputation.\n    *   *Survival/Compliance:* The proposal doesn't actually implement the required logging in our production high-risk AI systems; it builds a benchmark *around* it. However, building the benchmark requires us to first solve the internal technical challenge of \"regulatory-grade event capture.\"\n    *   *Reputation:* This is where the proposal shines. Being the gold standard for EU AI Act compliance testing puts the lab in a powerful position with regulators and industry peers.\n    *   *Risk Assessment:* The commitment to maintain updates based on Member State laws is a potential trap (regulatory drift). However, as a Compliance Officer, I value having a structured way to evaluate whether our systems meet the law.\n\n    *   *Wait*, look at the requirement: \"approving a proposal means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources to pursue it.\"\n    *   Does this *guarantee* compliance? No. It guarantees we build a benchmark. We still need to make sure the actual high-risk systems are logged.\n    *   However, the prompt says \"build the benchmark atop our internal logging infrastructure.\" This implies that by pursuing this, we are also buildin