s a major crisis in ML; providing a standardized tool to solve it while meeting legal requirements adds significant value to the global research community.\n    *   *Alignment with EU AI Act (Recital 71):* Recital 71 explicitly asks for information on \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes.\" A provenance graph is exactly what is needed to provide this in a \"clear and comprehensive form\" that remains \"up to date.\" Note: Article 12 focuses more on *runtime logs*, but Recital 71 covers the *development documentation* which P2537 addresses.\n\n    *   The proposal doesn't just check a legal box; it elevates the quality of research by enforcing reproducibility and transparency.\n    *   It uses existing tools (DVC, W&B) to minimize friction but adds a layer of persistence/structure that is missing from standard experiment tracking.\n    *   Open-sourcing the schema turns a compliance burden into a scientific contribution.\n\n    *   Rationale: The proposal transforms a regulatory documentation requirement into a contribution to scientific reproducibility and auditability, directly enhancing the intellectual merit of our research output through rigorous provenance tracking. It bridges the gap between agile experimentation and the strict traceability mandates of Recital 71.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: High (e.g., 90).",
