n leads to bottlenecks or disconnected documentation that doesn't reflect reality because the owners aren't the creators.\n    *   *The \"Named Owner\" aspect:* Accountability is good, but adding another layer of reporting (reporting to compliance) adds bureaucracy.\n\n    *   From a Lab Director's perspective: This looks like \"compliance bloat.\" It transforms a technical requirement into an organizational structure. The cost in headcount and the risk of slowing down R&D velocity are high. While the goal is necessary (compliance), the *method* (a dedicated office) is inefficient compared to integrating documentation into the existing CI/CD or development lifecycle. However, if we don't have a way to ensure this happens, regulators will shut us down.\n    *   *Wait*, looking at it strictly from \"resource-allocation efficiency\": Creating an entire *office* for documentation is generally inefficient in an R&D environment. It's better to automate the technical file generation or make it part of the engineer's definition of \"done.\"\n\n    *   Rationale: This proposal introduces significant administrative overhead and headcount costs by creating a separate organizational layer rather than integrating documentation into existing engineering workflows. While compliance is mandatory, the creation of a dedicated office risks diverting scarce resources away from scientific R&D without offering an efficient technical solution for record-keeping.\n    *   Decision: Reject. (Because it's too expensive/bureaucratic relative to the goal).\n    *   Promise: Low to Moderate. Maybe 30? It solves the problem but in a way that is hostile to R&D efficiency.\n\n    *   Actually, if I reject it, am I risking the lab? The prompt says \"approve every proposal that clears your bar.\" My bar is *resource-allocation efficiency*. A dedicated office for documentation is the opposite of efficient resource allocation; it's a bureaucratic fix.\n    *   Is there a way to do this without an offi