matically, it becomes a massive technical debt nightmare during audits or model refreshes.\n    *   A \"Living Technical-Documentation Lifecycle Programme\" provides a clear structure for how to handle the \"technical file\" mentioned in Recital 71.\n    *   While Article 12 is about *logs*, Recital 71 (which sets the context) specifically calls for the technical documentation to be kept up to date. The proposal addresses the non-logging part of the record-keeping requirement perfectly.\n    *   It doesn't require inventing new tech; it requires a process. As an engineer, I see this as a necessary \"maintenance\" cost to ensure that our prototypes actually make it to market without being blocked by legal/compliance.\n\n    *   Rationale: While adding administrative overhead, this systematizes the update of technical files for deployed systems, preventing documentation debt and ensuring compliance with Recital 71's requirements for up-to-date records.\n    *   Decision: Approve.\n    *   Promise: High (maybe 85). It's a straightforward process solution to a clear regulatory requirement.",
