those logs.\n    *   If I approve this, am I committing *implementation resources* (engineering time) to pursue it? The proposal says \"The compliance office drafts a binding internal policy.\" This implies the effort is primarily on the Compliance side, not the Engineering side.\n    *   Wait, if the compliance office does it, why is it in a list of proposals for implementation resources? Usually, policies dictate technical requirements. Without a clear policy on retention and access, engineers might build a logging system that is either too bloated (violating GDPR) or too sparse (violating AI Act).\n    *   However, the prompt asks me to judge based on *technical viability* and *speed/quality of prototyping*. A policy document doesn't \"build\" the system. But it defines the requirements for the technical solution.\n\n    *   *Critique from a Dev Eng perspective:* I don't build policies; I build systems. But if there's no policy, my prototypes will likely fail compliance audits later, forcing me to rewrite the logging architecture (reducing speed/quality). Therefore, having a clear specification of retention and access is a prerequisite for viable technical implementation.\n    *   *Counter-critique:* Is this an \"implementation\" proposal? It says \"Draft... Policy.\" This sounds like administrative work. If the lab's resources are \"scarce implementation resources,\" spending them on drafting policies might be seen as non-technical. But usually, these frameworks are essential for technical specs.\n    *   Let's look at Article 12 again. It requires the *system* to technically allow logging. P2653 ensures that once those logs exist, they are handled in a way that satisfies regulators and GDPR.\n\n    *   *Conclusion:* While it's a policy document, it provides the necessary constraints for the technical design of the logging system. Without it, engineering is guessing at retention periods and access levels, which leads to technical debt and rework. It supports the