ectively giving regulators (and competitors/litigants) a roadmap to every potential flaw in our high-risk systems.\n    *   *Market Reputation:* Position as an industry leader and contributor to standards. This is a strong positive for reputation.\n    *   *Resource Allocation:* It requires a dedicated interdisciplinary team and quarterly peer reviews. Is this the best use of resources to meet *compliance*? Article 12 mandates that we *have* logs; it doesn't mandate that we publish our documentation framework as an open-source standard for the world.\n\n    *   The proposal confuses \"record-keeping\" (logging events) with \"technical documentation standards\" (the template).\n    *   While Recital 71 mentions technical documentation, Article 12 is primarily about *logs*.\n    *   From a compliance and survival standpoint: Publicly open-sourcing the specific mapping of our high-risk systems' failure modes and rationales is an unacceptable legal and strategic risk (discovery goldmine for lawsuits).\n    *   It overreaches from \"meeting the law\" to \"setting a public standard,\" which consumes resources without directly solving the technical requirement of automatic logging.\n\n    *   Rationale: While this enhances reputation, it creates significant legal exposure by publicizing failure-mode analyses and diverts resources away from the mandatory technical implementation of automated logging required by Article 12.\n    *   Decision: reject.\n    *   Promise: Low (maybe 20-30) because while the *idea* of standardization is nice, doing it as a public artifact for high-risk AI failure modes is a compliance nightmare.",
