gn\" approach avoids the \"compliance debt\" of trying to retrofit logs onto a finished model (which is often impossible in AI).\n\n    *   *Institutional Survival:* High. Non-compliance with high-risk requirements can lead to massive fines (up to 7% or 35M EUR) and market bans.\n    *   *Regulatory Compliance:* Very strong. This proactive approach ensures the technical capabilities required by Art 12 are built-in, not bolted on.\n    *   *Market Reputation:* High. Being \"auditable\" and \"reproducible\" is a competitive advantage in highly regulated sectors (healthcare, finance, etc.).\n\n    *   *Rationale:* The proposal directly addresses the core requirements of Article 12 and Recital 71 by integrating automatic logging into the development lifecycle, mitigating the risk of non-compliance that occurs when traceability is attempted post-hoc. It aligns regulatory needs with research excellence (reproducibility), reducing friction between compliance and R&D.\n    *   *Decision:* Approve.\n    *   *Promise:* Very high. This is essentially a best-practice implementation for an AI lab facing the EU AI Act. 95/100.",
