tal phase, which is essential for later validation and intellectual merit.\n\n    *   The proposal makes compliance a \"path of least resistance\" rather than an administrative burden added at the end.\n    *   By embedding the regulatory requirements into the technical scaffolding, it protects the integrity of the research process (traceability) while freeing researchers to focus on the actual science.\n    *   It directly addresses Article 12 and Annex III 1(a).\n\n    *   Rationale: By integrating regulatory logging requirements directly into the R&D scaffolding, we protect the scientific integrity and reproducibility of our experiments without imposing an administrative tax on the creative prototyping phase. This ensures that traceability is a native property of our research rather than a retroactive compliance exercise.\n    *   Decision: Approve.\n    *   Promise: High (around 90). It's a pragmatic engineering solution to a regulatory burden that otherwise threatens research velocity and data rigor.",
